      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At lower pH levels, such as nearing 5.5, the hydrogen ion concentration increases, which actively protonates the nutrient ions in the soil solution, preventing them from binding tightly to soil particles or waiting into the lattice of humus compounds. This chemical process keeps iron and manganese in a highly soluble, free ionic or chelated state that plant roots can immediately absorb, rather than locking them up as insoluble oxides and hydroxides at higher pH values. As a result, the concentration of these micronutrients in the soil solution rises significantly, making them readily available for transcription into vine cells when needed to support enzymatic function and photosynthesis.",
